# Component styling files

Every component that needs more than a couple of inline classes gets a sibling
`ComponentName.styles.ts` (or `.tsx` only if the file exports JSX, e.g. a styled wrapper around a
shadcn primitive). The component file owns structure and behavior; the styles file owns every
Tailwind, `cva()`, and shadcn-className decision.

## What lives in `*.styles.ts(x)`

- `cva()` variant tables and their exported `VariantProps` type.
- `cn()`-composed class strings and any class-building helper functions.
- Style-only constants: variant maps, size/intent enums used purely for class selection.
- Wrapper components whose entire body is a shadcn primitive plus a fixed `className`/variant
  (only when the file needs `.tsx`).

```ts
// Button.styles.ts
import { cva, type VariantProps } from 'class-variance-authority';

export const buttonVariants = cva(
  'inline-flex items-center justify-center rounded-md text-sm font-medium transition-colors',
  {
    variants: {
      intent: {
        primary: 'bg-primary text-primary-foreground hover:bg-primary/90',
        danger: 'bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground hover:bg-destructive/90',
      },
      size: { sm: 'h-8 px-3', md: 'h-10 px-4' },
    },
    defaultVariants: { intent: 'primary', size: 'md' },
  },
);

export type ButtonVariants = VariantProps<typeof buttonVariants>;
```

```tsx
// Button.tsx
import { buttonVariants, type ButtonVariants } from './Button.styles';
import { cn } from '@/lib/utils';

type ButtonProps = React.ComponentProps<'button'> & ButtonVariants;

export const Button = ({ intent, size, className, ...props }: ButtonProps): JSX.Element => {
  return <button className={cn(buttonVariants({ intent, size }), className)} {...props} />;
};
```

## What stays out of the styles file

- Business logic, event handlers, hooks, data fetching — the styles file imports nothing from
  `application/`, `api/`, or `state/`.
- Structural JSX (layout, composition of children) — that is the component's job, styling only
  decides *how* a node looks, never *what* the tree contains.
- One-off layout classes that are not part of a variant axis (`flex`, `gap-2`, gaps between
  children) — those stay inline in the component's JSX; moving every class out is over-isolation
  that turns the styles file into an unreadable indirection layer.

## Contract between the two files

- The styles file exports plain functions/constants and, when variants exist, the derived
  `VariantProps` type — never a component that owns behavior.
- The component always merges the styles file's output with an incoming `className` via `cn()`,
  `className` last, so a caller can still override.
- Design tokens (`bg-primary`, `text-muted-foreground`, …) are the only color source; no raw hex or
  palette-step classes in either file. See the `tailwind-styles` skill for token and `cva()`
  authoring details, and `shadcn-usage` for primitive composition rules.
- Split only when it earns its keep: a component with two or three static classes and no variants
  does not need a `.styles.ts` file — inline `className` is clearer than a one-line indirection.
